# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Change

**`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 5+.** `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. You must replace the abort-based cancellation pattern entirely.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cancellation

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var waitTime = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != null)
        {
            if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(waitTime)) != _workerTask)
            {
                // Task didn't complete in time — log warning, but cannot force-kill
                Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no equivalent to `Abort()`. If code doesn't observe the token, it will hang. You *must* pass `CancellationToken` into all blocking calls. |
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()`** | If this wraps a `BlockingCollection`, use `collection.Take(token)`. If it's I/O, switch to an async API that accepts a token. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` gone** | No replacement needed — `OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception you catch and handle. |
| **`volatile bool` pattern** | Replace with `CancellationToken` — it's thread-safe, composable, and works with async APIs. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Store the `Task` and `await` it during shutdown to observe exceptions. Unobserved exceptions crash the process in some configurations. |
| **Dispose the CTS** | `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`. Leaking it leaks timer handles if you ever use `CancelAfter()`. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Code Async

For legacy synchronous code that can't accept a token:

```csharp
// Last resort: run on a dedicated thread, abandon if stuck
private void StopWithAbandon()
{
    _cts.Cancel();
    if (!_workerTask.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
    {
        // The thread is leaked but NOT aborted.
        // Log and accept the leak — or redesign the blocking call.
        Log.Error("Worker thread abandoned (leaked)");
    }
}
```

This is the **only** safe fallback — you leak the thread rather than corrupt state with an abort.
